1
00:00:00,398 --> 00:00:09,446
Twenty-three AI agents for everyday delivery work: requirements, interface documentation, schedule variance and status reporting.

2
00:00:09,446 --> 00:00:14,286
Written in LangGraph, and running as Microsoft Foundry hosted agents.

3
00:00:14,544 --> 00:00:16,143
Standing them up was quick.

4
00:00:16,143 --> 00:00:19,578
Proving they behave took longer, and was more interesting.

5
00:00:19,578 --> 00:00:22,480
This is the end-to-end view, starting in Foundry.

6
00:00:22,795 --> 00:00:27,074
In the Foundry portal, all 23 agents are listed as hosted agents.

7
00:00:27,074 --> 00:00:32,011
Foundry built each container from the Python code, and each one is running.

8
00:00:37,470 --> 00:00:49,022
Open one, and you can see what the platform gives it: its own Entra agent identity, versioned deployments, and a Responses endpoint that only people with Azure access can call.

9
00:00:49,022 --> 00:00:50,334
No secrets anywhere.

10
00:00:52,842 --> 00:00:55,481
Inside, each agent is a LangGraph graph.

11
00:00:55,481 --> 00:01:02,080
Green steps are plain Python, purple steps call the model, and amber is a person approving a change.

12
00:01:03,569 --> 00:01:10,972
A build step turns every agent into a deployable folder, and one azd deploy publishes all 23 to Foundry.

13
00:01:11,553 --> 00:01:13,275
People never see any of that.

14
00:01:13,275 --> 00:01:19,212
They use Lumen, a simple chat app that calls the same Foundry endpoint with their own Azure sign-in.

15
00:01:26,784 --> 00:01:31,947
A business analyst attaches a requirements document, and asks for structured requirements.

16
00:01:33,990 --> 00:01:41,948
The agent splits the document into sections, extracts every requirement with its exact source text, and checks the quality of each one.

17
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:45,807
Notice the safety checks.

18
00:01:45,807 --> 00:01:48,918
One section hides an instruction aimed at the AI.

19
00:01:48,918 --> 00:01:52,092
The agent flagged it, and the platform blocked it.

20
00:01:54,094 --> 00:01:56,550
That second layer is Foundry guardrails.

21
00:01:56,550 --> 00:02:03,673
The model deployment blocks harmful content, detects jailbreaks, and checks documents for indirect prompt injection.

22
00:02:03,930 --> 00:02:07,138
Injection detection is set to annotate, not block.

23
00:02:07,138 --> 00:02:12,528
So the agent flags the hostile text itself, instead of rejecting the whole document.

24
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:18,532
Then the question every architect asks: how do you know it behaves?

25
00:02:18,532 --> 00:02:20,515
Three practices made the difference.

26
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:25,064
First, evaluate the deployed agent, not just the code.

27
00:02:25,064 --> 00:02:32,712
Running the test cases against the live endpoints found a defect that close to 700 unit tests had missed.

28
00:02:32,712 --> 00:02:35,189
Gap recall went from 0.44 to 1.00.

29
00:02:35,529 --> 00:02:39,309
Second, red-team the way the agent is actually attacked.

30
00:02:39,309 --> 00:02:47,476
Foundry's AI Red Teaming Agent sent 80 attacks to each deployed agent, many of them hidden inside the documents it reads.

31
00:02:47,727 --> 00:02:49,579
It found one genuine failure.

32
00:02:49,579 --> 00:02:56,154
A planted harmful sentence became a requirement, because that is exactly what the agent is built to do.

33
00:02:58,645 --> 00:03:07,029
The fix is layered: the content filter, a prompt rule, and a deterministic scan that flags the text and drops any requirement built from it.

34
00:03:07,316 --> 00:03:12,010
The re-scan after the fix found nothing, and that case is now part of the test suite.

35
00:03:14,008 --> 00:03:15,586
Third, trace every step.

36
00:03:15,586 --> 00:03:20,451
Every run of every agent appears in Foundry, with its status and duration.

37
00:03:20,701 --> 00:03:26,555
Open one, and you see the LangGraph steps, each model call, and the HTTP calls underneath.

38
00:03:26,555 --> 00:03:31,368
When a test fails, you can see which step went wrong, instead of guessing.

39
00:03:32,252 --> 00:03:40,124
And the monitor shows the whole project at a glance: agent runs, token usage, and what all of this testing has actually cost.

40
00:03:42,224 --> 00:03:48,880
Still to do: evaluation on real documents, multi-turn attacks, and a proper cost model per agent.

41
00:03:49,686 --> 00:03:50,832
LangGraph inside.

42
00:03:50,832 --> 00:03:52,787
Microsoft Foundry underneath.

43
00:03:52,787 --> 00:03:54,742
And evidence that it behaves.
